WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.776
Subagents: isolation, restriction, and a fresh pair of eyes.

2
00:00:04.056 --> 00:00:08.899
A subagent runs its own loop in its own context window and returns a summary.

3
00:00:09.179 --> 00:00:11.803
Custom subagents are Markdown files in.

4
00:00:12.083 --> 00:00:15.347
claude/agents/ (project) or ~/.

5
00:00:15.627 --> 00:00:33.867
claude/agents/ (personal), with frontmatter that requires a name and a description (which tells Claude when to delegate) and optionally sets tools, disallowedTools, model, permissionMode, skills to preload, isolation: worktree to work in a temporary git worktree, maxTurns, and more.

6
00:00:34.147 --> 00:00:38.627
Built-in subagents include a read-only Explore agent and a Plan agent.

7
00:00:38.907 --> 00:00:43.451
The best-practices doc is unusually candid about what subagents are for.

8
00:00:43.731 --> 00:00:58.856
They pay off for running independent work at the same time, keeping noisy output (a full test run, a wide search) out of your main conversation, getting a reviewer that hasn’t seen how the code was written, and giving a task fewer tools than the main session.

9
00:00:59.136 --> 00:01:05.003
They don’t make answers smarter: by default it’s the same model (or a smaller one) on the same task.

10
00:01:05.283 --> 00:01:11.896
They cost tokens, since each sends its own requests, and a fresh one may be slower because it must rebuild context.

11
00:01:12.176 --> 00:01:13.669
The restriction is real.

12
00:01:13.949 --> 00:01:21.693
A subagent with tools: Read, Grep, Glob can’t edit anything, and Claude Code enforces that rather than asking nicely.

13
00:01:21.973 --> 00:01:25.472
Pair it with model: haiku for simple jobs to cut cost.

14
00:01:25.752 --> 00:01:33.496
This makes the classic pattern cheap to set up: an implementer in the main session and a read-only reviewer with a short, specific brief.
